  "title": "When intermittent composition is a law",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Chains of three rings joined by two shared contacts, as in PROCESS-CHAIN-20261108-001; the chain composes at lag T when its far contact c2 binds the end rings A and C at T. (1) The composing lags are eventually periodic with the period P of the chain's T-step maps, and the chain observed only at the lags of one residue modulo P composes at all of them or at none: intermittent composition is persistent composition of a strobed chain, and the strobe's phase is the only input the chain does not fix. (2) For linear or affine rules with affine contact laws, the chain composes at lag T exactly when c2 depends on both end rings at T, so its composing lags are the arrival lags of both ends' influence. (3) For any chain, composition at T implies that c2 depends on both end rings at T.",
